<ArticleLayout
  title="Textiles, Skills & Enterprise Hubs"
  description="A cotton-to-fabric value chain from pilot to industrial scale, plus carpentry, woodworking and diamond beneficiation hubs in Sierra Leone."
>
  <p>
    Practical skills only translate into income when there is a market
    to sell into. This area builds full value chains and enterprise
    hubs around real trades, from raw material through to a finished,
    sellable product.
  </p>

  <h2>What this covers</h2>
  <p>
    Fibre to Fabric takes cotton through yarn, fabric and tailoring in
    a staged pathway: a lean proof-of-concept, a scale-up phase
    expanding processing and enterprise capacity, a broader value-chain
    infrastructure phase, and an industrial-scale textile facility with
    a genuine job-creation pathway at the far end.
  </p>
  <p>
    A carpentry and woodworking skills hub gives trainees workshop
    access, machine and tool rental, and starter tool kits so training
    converts directly into a small business. A diamond cutting and
    polishing workshop builds local beneficiation capacity and skilled
    jobs around a resource that, in Sierra Leone, has long been
    exported unprocessed.
  </p>

  <h2>How we work</h2>
  <p>
    Every hub is designed around a staged path from training to
    tool access to independent enterprise, so a trainee&rsquo;s next
    step is always clear and the hub itself builds toward local
    ownership over time.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
